<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry S. Truman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Truman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harry S. Truman was the 33rd president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1945 to 1953. Born in rural Missouri, he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as an artillery captain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">U.S. Senate</a> in 1934 and, as vice president, succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> on his death in April 1945. He authorized the first and only use of nuclear weapons in war, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a>, established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a> and supported the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> to contain Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. He presided over the onset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Airlift</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, and issued Executive Order 9981, which desegregated the armed forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman.jpg" alt="Harry S. Truman" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Archives and Records Administration. Office of Presidential Libraries. Harry S. Truman Library. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TRUMAN_58-766-06_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harry S. Truman was born in Lamar, Missouri, on 8 May 1884, the oldest child of John Anderson Truman and Martha Ellen Young Truman. His middle initial honours both his grandfathers and is not an abbreviation of one name. His father was a farmer and livestock dealer, and the family settled in Independence, Missouri, when Harry was six. He was refused an appointment to West Point because of poor eyesight and served in the Missouri National Guard from 1905 to 1911.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> on 6 April 1917, Truman rejoined his battery and fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as a captain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">Field Artillery</a>. In 1919 he married Bess Wallace. He and Edward Jacobson opened a haberdashery in Kansas City, which went bankrupt in the recession of 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Truman was elected a judge of Jackson County in 1922 and a senator for Missouri in 1934. From 1940 to 1944 he gained national prominence as chairman of the Truman Committee, which aimed to reduce waste and inefficiency in wartime contracts. He was nominated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>'s running mate, though reluctant, and was sworn in as vice president on 20 January 1945. He became president on Roosevelt's death in April. Only then was he told about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a> and the atomic bomb.</p><p>As president Truman authorized the use of nuclear weapons against the Japanese cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a>. He supported the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and presided over the onset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> in 1947 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Airlift</a> in 1948. When Congress refused his proposal for comprehensive civil rights legislation, he issued orders that prohibited discrimination in federal agencies and desegregated the armed forces. He won the 1948 election against Thomas E. Dewey. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> lasted from 1950 to 1953. Corruption in parts of his administration, which did not implicate Truman himself, became a campaign issue in 1952, and he chose not to run again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In retirement Truman founded his presidential library and published his memoirs. He was admitted to hospital in Kansas City with pneumonia on 5 December 1972, developed multiple organ failure and died on 26 December, aged 88. When he left office in 1953 his administration was heavily criticised, but public feeling towards him grew warmer over the following decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harry S. Truman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1948 President <b>Harry S. Truman</b> ordered the desegregation of the armed forces, and the Cold War made segregation an embarrassment for a country seeking allies in Asia and Africa.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Harry Truman</b> became president and presided over the end of the war in Europe and the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In his final State of the Union message, <b>Harry Truman</b> disclosed that the US had developed the hydrogen bomb, tested in November 1952.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-7">7 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Johnson signed the Social Security Amendments in Independence, Missouri, with <b>Harry Truman</b> beside him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Asking Congress for aid to Greece and Turkey, <b>Harry Truman</b> pledged to support 'free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Harry S Truman Birthplace State Historic Site, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Research Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.5,-94.27&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Harry S Truman Birthplace State Historic Site on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2194 814 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harry S. Truman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2644 1084h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2644 1084h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.5,-94.27&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The seeds of totalitarian regimes are nurtured by misery and want. They spread and grow in the evil soil of poverty and strife. They reach their full growth when the hope of a people for a better life has died.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to Congress (the Truman Doctrine), 12 March 1947 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Truman_Doctrine" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I believe that it must be the policy of the United States to support free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or by outside pressures.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to Congress (the Truman Doctrine), 12 March 1947 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Truman_Doctrine" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Communism is based on the belief that man is so weak and inadequate that he is unable to govern himself, and therefore requires the rule of strong masters.&#8221;</p><footer>Inaugural Address (1949) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/925/pg925-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
